Vector is a legacy List implementation in Java that is thread-safe by default.
It belongs to the old Java 1.0 era and is part of the Collections Framework, but today it is rarely recommended.

Vector is:

✔ A resizable array (like ArrayList)
✔ Synchronized (thread-safe) by default
✔ Allows duplicate & null values
✔ Maintains insertion order

public synchronized boolean add(E e) - always synchornized

Vector is outdated due to performance issue

So instead of:
   Vector<String> v = new Vector<>();
Use:
  List<String> list = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>());
OR
  List<String> list = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();